\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.2}A version is a set of edits, and the files are rebuilt from it}{6}{subsection.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:sets}{{4.2}{6}{A version is a set of edits, and the files are rebuilt from it}{subsection.4.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces What one save writes down, what a version is, and where the bytes in a file come from. A save writes one record per changed function and files the records under a guess at the request they served, which the developer corrects with one command (left). A version is the set of those records, so taking out the price cache is subtracting six records from eighteen, and two rules decide which sets are allowed (middle). The files are rebuilt from the set with the text between functions kept exactly, so the same set always produces the same bytes (right). Two kinds of mark in the right-hand column are the two consequences \textsf  {sgt}{} reports about this revert and does not act on. The hollow triangle on \fsym {api.py::handle} means the retry policy edited that function after the cache did, so \textsf  {sgt}{} took the cache's lines back out of the current text and the developer's test suite decides whether the result stands. The open circles are three functions that still call \fsym {cache.py::get\_cached}, which \textsf  {sgt}{} names and leaves alone. \fsym {db.py::query} is the one function that goes back to text it had earlier in the day.}}{7}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:model}{{3}{7}{\FigThreeCaption }{figure.caption.6}{}}
\citation{zimmermann2004,ying2004}
\citation{traag2019}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Two people change \sym {api.py::handle} from the version both of them last saved, one taking three lines off the top of it and one adding a line to the logging block at its end. Git's merge of the two succeeds and prints nothing, and nobody has run the function it produces in that state. \textsf  {sgt}{} records both versions, includes neither, and names the function, which is the only refusal in \textsf  {sgt}{} that can stop work that would have been correct.}}{8}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fork}{{4}{8}{\FigForkCaption }{figure.caption.7}{}}
